WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
You know, usually when we talk about a medical diagnosis,

2
00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.280
there's this expectation of just absolute precision.

3
00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:08.120
Oh, sure.

4
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:09.680
Like it feels like engineering or something.

5
00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:11.160
You break your arm, you get an X-ray,

6
00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:12.160
and it shows that, you know,

7
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.520
that jagged white line on the radius bone.

8
00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.560
Right, it's binary, it's incredibly clean.

9
00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.600
Exactly, the doctor just points to the screen

10
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.040
and says, there it is, broken.

11
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:21.880
And we like that.

12
00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:23.880
I mean, inherently we find it comforting

13
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.400
when things are visible and perfectly categorized.

14
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.560
We wanna know exactly what we're fighting.

15
00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.080
Yeah, but then, and this is why we're here today,

16
00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.800
you step into the world of human behavior.

17
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.080
You walk into a clinical psychology clinic

18
00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:38.320
and suddenly that X-ray machine is,

19
00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.520
well, it's entirely useless.

20
00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:41.560
Completely useless.

21
00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:44.960
The diagnostic landscape is honestly incredibly murky.

22
00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.520
So, welcome to today's deep dive.

23
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.440
If you're a learner, and I know you are

24
00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.600
because you're sitting here with us right now,

25
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.320
you've probably wondered how professionals

26
00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.640
actually make sense of the human mind without that X-ray.

27
00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:58.760
It's a huge challenge.

28
00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:01.880
Right, so today we are cracking open

29
00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:06.080
what is basically a masterclass in reading human behavior.

30
00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.280
We've got this stack of clinical test prep transcripts,

31
00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.600
specifically pass-ready prep by Keek,

32
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.680
which are designed for mental health counselors.

33
00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:17.640
Yeah, and the goal here isn't to study for an exam,

34
00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.040
obviously. No, definitely not.

35
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.280
We're trying to decode how professionals

36
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:23.640
actually distinguish between these near-identical

37
00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.040
psychological states.

38
00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:26.560
How do they keep people safe?

39
00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:28.320
What actually drives human behavior?

40
00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.840
It really is a fascinating framework to look at.

41
00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.400
And I have to say, seeing you set up there

42
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:33.600
for this deep dive,

43
00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.840
I mean, you've got this expansive,

44
00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:38.800
warmly lit library backdrop.

45
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:39.640
Oh, thanks.

46
00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.040
Yeah, with those intricate behavioral charts behind you,

47
00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:43.960
it's very fitting because the truth is,

48
00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:46.560
this entire diagnostic manual we're exploring today,

49
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.280
it's ultimately about drawing boundaries.

50
00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:49.120
New boundaries, yeah.

51
00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:50.640
Right, it's an attempt to figure out

52
00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.840
where normal human distress ends,

53
00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:54.920
you know, and a clinical disorder actually begins.

54
00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:57.720
And that requires an incredible amount of precision

55
00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:59.360
to navigate that gray area.

56
00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.280
Okay, let's untack this.

57
00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.480
I wanna start with the most common internal experiences

58
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.960
that, well, pretty much all of us recognize, right?

59
00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.040
Anxiety and depression.

60
00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:08.880
The big two.

61
00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:09.840
Yeah.

62
00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:11.760
When I first looked at this source material,

63
00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.000
I just assumed anxiety was anxiety,

64
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.480
like you feel nervous, your heart beats fast,

65
00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:19.800
you sweat, but professionals actually map this out

66
00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.800
by looking for a specific target, right?

67
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:23.520
Like the target of the fear.

68
00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:24.360
Exactly.

69
00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:27.640
The anxiety family is entirely driven by fear,

70
00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:29.920
that is the biological engine running the whole thing.

71
00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:30.760
Right.

72
00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.880
But you cannot diagnose the specific disorder

73
00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:36.080
just by looking at the physical panic.

74
00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:37.840
The diagnosis entirely depends

75
00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:39.280
on what that fear is pointed at.

76
00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.200
You really have to find the invisible target.

77
00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.760
Okay, let me try to map this out based on what I read.

78
00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:45.560
So if the worry is just spread out

79
00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:46.880
across a million different domains,

80
00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:49.320
like I'm worried about my job, my health,

81
00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.920
whether I left the stove on, my kids' grades.

82
00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:53.000
Just a constant hum.

83
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:55.400
Yeah, this persistent hum that lasts for months,

84
00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:57.240
that's generalized anxiety disorder.

85
00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:58.080
Well, GED.

86
00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.680
But what if the fear is pointed

87
00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:01.040
at something really specific,

88
00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:03.000
like being around a crowd of people?

89
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:03.840
Yeah.

90
00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:04.760
Well, this is where the clinician

91
00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:06.120
has to dig a little deeper,

92
00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:08.920
because just saying fear of crowds

93
00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:10.280
isn't enough information.

94
00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.760
You have to ask, why are you afraid of the crime?

95
00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:13.920
Ah, okay.

96
00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:17.600
Right, so if your fear is pointed at being judged

97
00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:20.160
like at the scrutiny or the potential

98
00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.840
for negative evaluation by those people,

99
00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:25.080
then you're looking at social anxiety disorder.

100
00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:26.800
Okay, but what if it's not about being judged?

101
00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:29.120
What if you just feel trapped in that crowd?

102
00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:31.520
Then you're likely looking at agoraphobia.

103
00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:32.360
Oh, wow.

104
00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:33.960
Yeah, in that case, the fear is pointed

105
00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.800
at being trapped in a situation where,

106
00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:39.360
you know, escape is physically difficult,

107
00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.680
or where help might be totally unavailable

108
00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:42.680
if you start to panic.

109
00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:43.840
So the behavior is the same,

110
00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:45.120
but the reason is different.

111
00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:45.960
Exactly.

112
00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:47.280
A person with social anxiety

113
00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:48.560
and a person with agoraphobia

114
00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:51.840
might both avoid going to a crowded shopping mall.

115
00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.240
So the presenting behavior looks totally identical,

116
00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:57.080
but the underlying motive,

117
00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:00.320
the engine of that avoidance is completely different.

118
00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:02.400
So anxiety is basically a smoke alarm.

119
00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:03.240
I like that.

120
00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:05.040
Yeah, so generalized anxiety

121
00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:07.160
is when the alarm is just constantly humming

122
00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:09.360
in the background for absolutely everything.

123
00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:11.480
Social anxiety is when it only blares

124
00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:14.000
because it detects other people judging you.

125
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.440
Yes, and to finish that analogy,

126
00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.440
panic disorder is when you are actually

127
00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:20.120
terrified of the alarm itself.

128
00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:21.240
Wait, really?

129
00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:22.560
Terrified of the alarm.

130
00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:23.640
Exactly.

131
00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:24.560
In panic disorder,

132
00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:28.360
the fear is pointed at the next unexpected surge of panic.

133
00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:30.760
So you're afraid of being afraid?

134
00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:31.880
Basically, yeah.

135
00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:34.120
The person becomes hyper-focused

136
00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:35.880
on their own physiological sensations.

137
00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:37.680
So like a slight change in heart rate,

138
00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:38.520
a little shortness of breath.

139
00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:39.720
They're again, they spiral.

140
00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:41.400
Right, because they are terrified

141
00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.000
of having another panic attack.

142
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.880
The target of the fear is the fear itself.

143
00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:49.360
What's fascinating here is how the diagnostic criteria

144
00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:51.520
force the clinician to look right past

145
00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:52.520
the presenting symptom.

146
00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:54.480
Because treating the symptom isn't enough.

147
00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:56.200
No, you can't just treat the avoidance.

148
00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:57.960
You have to treat the specific fear

149
00:04:57.960 --> 00:04:59.400
that's actually driving it.

150
00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:00.680
That makes total sense.

151
00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:02.720
And it seems like the stakes for finding

152
00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:05.040
that underlying engine get incredibly high

153
00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:08.120
when we move from anxiety over to mood disorders.

154
00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:08.960
Oh, absolutely.

155
00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:11.880
Especially that boundary between unipolar-depressive

156
00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:15.040
like major depressive disorder and bipolar disorder.

157
00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:16.920
The source material made a huge deal out of this.

158
00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:19.960
It is a matter of absolute critical safety.

159
00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:21.960
I mean, when a client comes into a clinic,

160
00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:23.840
they're almost always coming in

161
00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:26.120
during the low, the depressive phase.

162
00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:26.960
Right.

163
00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:27.800
Think about it.

164
00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:29.280
Nobody schedules an emergency therapy appointment

165
00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:32.440
because they feel invincible, need zero sleep

166
00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:35.480
and have this rush of brilliant racing thoughts.

167
00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:37.520
Yeah, they only come in when they crash.

168
00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:38.800
So they sit down on the couch

169
00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:40.920
and they present looking exactly like someone

170
00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:43.000
with standard major depressive disorder.

171
00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:44.640
But here's the danger.

172
00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:46.800
If you just look at that presentation

173
00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:48.760
and you assume it's unipolar depression

174
00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:52.920
and then treat it with a standard SSRI antidepressant,

175
00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:54.600
you can cause profound harm.

176
00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:57.560
Because SSRI's flood the brain with serotonin, right?

177
00:05:57.560 --> 00:06:00.120
So if they actually have an underlying bipolar disorder

178
00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:02.120
that you missed, what actually happens?

179
00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:04.520
It acts like pouring rocket fuel onto a latent engine.

180
00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:05.360
Oh my God.

181
00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:07.560
Yeah, an antidepressant given alone

182
00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:09.520
to someone with bipolar disorder

183
00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:11.080
can literally flip that patient

184
00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:14.160
into a full dangerous manic episode.

185
00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:15.400
That is terrifying.

186
00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:16.320
It really is.

187
00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:18.440
They go from being profoundly depressed

188
00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:22.520
to dangerously impulsive, detached from reality

189
00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:24.400
or unable to sleep for days.

190
00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:26.480
So just by giving them the standard treatment,

191
00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:27.720
you make it worse.

192
00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:29.080
Much worse.

193
00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:31.600
It is the single most consequential danger

194
00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:33.440
of misreading mood.

195
00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.280
You treat the symptom directly in front of you

196
00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:36.760
without checking the history

197
00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:38.800
and you trigger a major crisis.

198
00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:39.640
Wow.

199
00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:42.480
That is why the cardinal rule in diagnostics

200
00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:44.040
is that you must actively hunt

201
00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:46.640
for a history of mania or hypomania

202
00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:49.200
with every single depressed client.

203
00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:50.280
You have to know the history

204
00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:51.640
before you touch the chemistry.

205
00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:52.480
Well said.

206
00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:54.520
Which actually transitions us perfectly

207
00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:57.160
to another boundary that really trips people up time.

208
00:06:57.160 --> 00:06:58.760
The way timelines dictate

209
00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:00.720
whether someone is detached from reality.

210
00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.600
Yes, the psychotic spectrum.

211
00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:03.440
Right.

212
00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:04.720
When it comes to the psychotic spectrum,

213
00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:07.360
it seems like the diagnosis is literally just a clock.

