The diagnostic criteria require

555
00:18:47.440 --> 00:18:50.160
that the symptoms of inattention or hyperactivity

556
00:18:50.160 --> 00:18:51.960
appear before age 12,

557
00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:54.000
and crucially, they must be present

558
00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:56.280
in two or more settings.

559
00:18:56.280 --> 00:18:59.020
Why is that multiple setting rule so important?

560
00:18:59.020 --> 00:19:02.360
Because ADHD is a neurodevelopmental disorder.

561
00:19:02.360 --> 00:19:04.600
It is a structural and functional difference

562
00:19:04.600 --> 00:19:06.040
in how the brain operates.

563
00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:09.140
And a brain doesn't completely change how it operates

564
00:19:09.140 --> 00:19:11.040
just because you walk through a different door.

565
00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:12.240
Ah, okay.

566
00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:14.920
So if a kid is heavily distracted and impulsive at school

567
00:19:14.920 --> 00:19:17.920
but perfectly focused, organized, and calm at home.

568
00:19:17.920 --> 00:19:20.760
Then you likely don't have a neurodevelopmental disorder.

569
00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:21.600
What do you have then?

570
00:19:21.600 --> 00:19:24.120
You might have a kid who is being bullied

571
00:19:24.120 --> 00:19:27.280
or has a learning disability specific to that classroom

572
00:19:27.280 --> 00:19:29.400
or is dealing with anxiety triggered

573
00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:31.040
by that specific teacher.

574
00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:33.260
If the symptoms don't exist in two settings,

575
00:19:33.260 --> 00:19:34.540
you have to look at the environment,

576
00:19:34.540 --> 00:19:36.020
not just the child's brain.

577
00:19:36.020 --> 00:19:38.020
That is such a crucial distinction.

578
00:19:38.020 --> 00:19:40.420
And jumping to the other end of the lifespan,

579
00:19:40.420 --> 00:19:42.460
the distinction between delirium and dementia

580
00:19:42.460 --> 00:19:45.540
in older adults is another major safety boundary.

581
00:19:45.540 --> 00:19:48.340
Dementia, like Alzheimer's, is insidious.

582
00:19:48.340 --> 00:19:50.500
It creeps in slowly over years

583
00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:53.300
and progressively degrades cognition.

584
00:19:53.300 --> 00:19:55.180
But delirium is acute.

585
00:19:55.180 --> 00:19:58.260
Yes, delirium comes on rapidly over hours or days

586
00:19:58.260 --> 00:20:00.380
and cognition severely fluctuates.

587
00:20:00.380 --> 00:20:02.620
And the key here is the mechanism.

588
00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:04.240
Delirium is usually caused

589
00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:06.960
by a reversible underlying medical issue.

590
00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:08.120
Like what kind of issue?

591
00:20:08.120 --> 00:20:09.460
Well, in elderly patients,

592
00:20:09.460 --> 00:20:11.120
a simple urinary tract infection

593
00:20:11.120 --> 00:20:13.480
or a bad reaction to a new medication

594
00:20:13.480 --> 00:20:16.720
can cause sudden profound confusion and hallucinations

595
00:20:16.720 --> 00:20:19.320
that look terrifyingly like severe dementia.

596
00:20:19.320 --> 00:20:21.240
But if a clinician or a doctor mistakes

597
00:20:21.240 --> 00:20:24.000
that sudden delirium for a natural progressive decline,

598
00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:25.280
they might just shrug and say,

599
00:20:25.280 --> 00:20:26.360
well, they're getting older.

600
00:20:26.360 --> 00:20:27.680
And they miss the chance to cure it

601
00:20:27.680 --> 00:20:29.720
with a simple course of antibiotics.

602
00:20:29.720 --> 00:20:31.680
Delirium is a medical emergency

603
00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:33.740
hiding as cognitive decline.

604
00:20:33.740 --> 00:20:35.420
The theme of crisis and safety

605
00:20:35.420 --> 00:20:37.580
is just everywhere in this framework.

606
00:20:37.580 --> 00:20:39.920
Let's talk about assessing suicide risk.

607
00:20:39.920 --> 00:20:42.700
When a clinician is sitting with someone in crisis,

608
00:20:42.700 --> 00:20:44.380
they use structured protocols

609
00:20:44.380 --> 00:20:46.900
like the Columbia Suicide Severity Rating Scale.

610
00:20:46.900 --> 00:20:48.380
The CSSRS, yes.

611
00:20:48.380 --> 00:20:51.660
Right, they assess the ideation, the plan,

612
00:20:51.660 --> 00:20:54.520
the intent, the access to lethal means.

613
00:20:54.520 --> 00:20:56.780
But I was really surprised by what the standard is

614
00:20:56.780 --> 00:20:58.640
for actually preventing it.

615
00:20:58.640 --> 00:21:00.060
Because for decades, you know,

616
00:21:00.080 --> 00:21:02.400
we've heard about no suicide contracts.

617
00:21:02.400 --> 00:21:03.880
You make the patient sign a paper

618
00:21:03.880 --> 00:21:05.840
promising they won't hurt themselves.

619
00:21:05.840 --> 00:21:08.480
And those contracts are not evidence-based.

620
00:21:08.480 --> 00:21:10.640
In fact, they can be deeply harmful.

621
00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:12.800
Why though, wouldn't a promise help?

622
00:21:12.800 --> 00:21:14.880
Because a contract is coercive.

623
00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:18.060
It exists primarily to protect the clinician's liability,

624
00:21:18.060 --> 00:21:19.840
not to empower the patient.

625
00:21:19.840 --> 00:21:21.240
It essentially tells the patient,

626
00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:23.860
promise you won't do this, or else.

627
00:21:23.860 --> 00:21:27.120
The evidence-based standard is a collaborative safety plan.

628
00:21:27.120 --> 00:21:29.520
A safety plan, where they sit down together

629
00:21:29.540 --> 00:21:31.580
and map out a step-by-step strategy

630
00:21:31.580 --> 00:21:34.620
for what the patient can actually do when a crisis hits.

631
00:21:34.620 --> 00:21:35.500
Yes.

632
00:21:35.500 --> 00:21:38.820
It sounds more like, when the pain hits an eight out of 10,

633
00:21:38.820 --> 00:21:40.320
I will call this friend.

634
00:21:40.320 --> 00:21:42.760
If they don't answer, I will try this coping skill.

635
00:21:42.760 --> 00:21:45.340
If that fails, I will call this hotline.

636
00:21:45.340 --> 00:21:46.600
So it's actionable.

637
00:21:46.600 --> 00:21:47.440
Exactly.

638
00:21:47.440 --> 00:21:50.400
It gives the patient agency and concrete tools

639
00:21:50.400 --> 00:21:52.540
rather than a coercive demand.

640
00:21:52.540 --> 00:21:55.580
The framework also carefully separates suicidal intent

641
00:21:55.580 --> 00:21:58.860
from non-suicidal self-injury, or an SSI.

642
00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:01.060
When someone is cutting or burning themselves,

643
00:22:01.060 --> 00:22:02.680
it's natural for people to panic

644
00:22:02.680 --> 00:22:04.420
and assume it's a suicide attempt.

645
00:22:04.420 --> 00:22:07.080
But NSSI has a completely different function.

646
00:22:07.080 --> 00:22:10.560
The function of NSSI is almost always a motion regulation.

647
00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:13.280
It is a deeply maladaptive physical way

648
00:22:13.280 --> 00:22:15.400
to deal with unbearable emotional numbness

649
00:22:15.400 --> 00:22:16.840
or overwhelming distress.

650
00:22:16.840 --> 00:22:18.120
So it's not about dying?

651
00:22:18.120 --> 00:22:18.960
No.

652
00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:21.040
The physical pain is used to ground the person

653
00:22:21.040 --> 00:22:23.000
or release internal pressure.

654
00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:24.960
It is not an attempt to end a life.

655
00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:27.320
You have to assess the function of the behavior

656
00:22:27.340 --> 00:22:28.660
rather than just panicking

657
00:22:28.660 --> 00:22:30.300
and immediately hospitalizing someone,

658
00:22:30.300 --> 00:22:32.300
which honestly could traumatize them further.

659
00:22:32.300 --> 00:22:33.980
Which leads right into the ethical rules

660
00:22:33.980 --> 00:22:35.460
of engagement for clinicians.

661
00:22:35.460 --> 00:22:38.500
Confidentiality is the absolute bedrock of therapy.

662
00:22:38.500 --> 00:22:39.340
It has to be.

663
00:22:39.340 --> 00:22:40.900
But it has hard limits.

664
00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:43.100
The Terasov Duty is the most famous one.

665
00:22:43.100 --> 00:22:44.860
The duty to warn and protect.

666
00:22:44.860 --> 00:22:45.700
Right.

667
00:22:45.700 --> 00:22:48.140
If a client makes a specific credible threat

668
00:22:48.140 --> 00:22:51.740
of imminent violence against a specific identifiable victim,

669
00:22:51.740 --> 00:22:54.460
the clinician is legally and ethically bound

670
00:22:54.460 --> 00:22:58.120
to break confidentiality to warn that victim

671
00:22:58.120 --> 00:22:59.500
and notify the police.

672
00:22:59.500 --> 00:23:01.400
But it has to be a specific threat, right?

673
00:23:01.400 --> 00:23:03.940
Like the clinician can't break confidentiality

674
00:23:03.940 --> 00:23:05.960
just because a client is vaguely venting saying,

675
00:23:05.960 --> 00:23:07.080
oh, I hate my boss.

676
00:23:07.080 --> 00:23:07.960
I wish you were dead.

677
00:23:07.960 --> 00:23:08.800
Correct.

678
00:23:08.800 --> 00:23:10.440
Vague venting is not actionable.

679
00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:13.360
The threat must be imminent and targeted.

680
00:23:13.360 --> 00:23:15.920
And similar, very strict thresholds apply

681
00:23:15.920 --> 00:23:18.600
to mandated reporting of child or elder abuse.

682
00:23:18.600 --> 00:23:19.800
For mandated reporting,

683
00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:23.480
the threshold is reasonable suspicion, not concrete proof.

684
00:23:23.480 --> 00:23:27.100
The rule is basically you report, you do not investigate.

685
00:23:27.100 --> 00:23:28.220
Let me try an analogy here.

686
00:23:28.220 --> 00:23:29.060
Let's hear it.

687
00:23:29.060 --> 00:23:32.060
So mandated reporting is like smelling smoke coming

688
00:23:32.060 --> 00:23:33.740
from under a locked door.

689
00:23:33.740 --> 00:23:36.940
My job is just to pull the fire alarm and wait outside.

690
00:23:36.940 --> 00:23:39.580
If I kick open the door to investigate the fire myself

691
00:23:39.580 --> 00:23:41.920
to gather evidence, I'm actually doing the wrong job

692
00:23:41.920 --> 00:23:43.260
and wasting precious time.

693
00:23:43.260 --> 00:23:45.340
That is an excellent way to conceptualize it.

694
00:23:45.340 --> 00:23:47.000
You are not a detective.

695
00:23:47.000 --> 00:23:49.220
If you delay protection to play investigator,

696
00:23:49.220 --> 00:23:52.260
you risk the child's safety and you fail ethically.

697
00:23:53.120 --> 00:23:55.360
But the nuance is in the word reasonable.

698
00:23:55.360 --> 00:23:56.600
You don't pull the fire alarm just

699
00:23:56.600 --> 00:23:58.320
because someone mentions the word fire.

700
00:23:58.320 --> 00:23:59.360
Right, you have to assess.

701
00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:01.720
You assess the situation, apply the code,

702
00:24:01.720 --> 00:24:03.320
consult with the supervisor

703
00:24:03.320 --> 00:24:06.920
and document every single step of your reasoning.

704
00:24:06.920 --> 00:24:09.640
The measured answer is always the right one in ethics.

705
00:24:09.640 --> 00:24:11.420
Okay, we've covered the engines of fear,

706
00:24:11.420 --> 00:24:14.100
the timelines of reality, physical emergencies

707
00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:15.880
and ethical crises.

708
00:24:15.880 --> 00:24:18.840
How does a clinician capture all of this complexity

709
00:24:18.840 --> 00:24:19.940
in real time?

710
00:24:19.960 --> 00:24:22.320
The tool they use is called the mental status exam

711
00:24:22.320 --> 00:24:23.400
or MSE, right?

712
00:24:23.400 --> 00:24:26.840
Yes, the MSE is the ultimate clinical photograph.

713
00:24:26.840 --> 00:24:29.120
It is a structured observation of exactly

714
00:24:29.120 --> 00:24:30.800
how the client presents in the room

715
00:24:30.800 --> 00:24:32.560
during that specific session.

716
00:24:32.560 --> 00:24:35.360
It is not their history, it is the now.

717
00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:37.560
And the MSE features a few distinctions

718
00:24:37.560 --> 00:24:38.720
that are famously tricky.

719
00:24:38.720 --> 00:24:40.600
First is mood versus affect.

720
00:24:40.600 --> 00:24:42.680
I love the way this is conceptualized in the source.

721
00:24:42.680 --> 00:24:45.300
Mood is the climate and affect is the weather.

722
00:24:45.300 --> 00:24:47.640
That's a perfect shared shorthand.

723
00:24:47.640 --> 00:24:49.280
Mood is subjective.

724
00:24:49.300 --> 00:24:51.480
It is the overall climate the client tells you

725
00:24:51.480 --> 00:24:52.900
they're experiencing.

726
00:24:52.900 --> 00:24:55.740
I have been profoundly depressed for weeks.

727
00:24:55.740 --> 00:24:58.540
But the affect is the weather outside the window right now.

728
00:24:58.540 --> 00:25:00.620
It's subjective, it's the emotional expression

729
00:25:00.620 --> 00:25:02.020
the clinician observes in the room.

730
00:25:02.020 --> 00:25:05.920
Right, and you are watching for incongruence.

731
00:25:05.920 --> 00:25:09.140
If a client tells you their climate is devastatingly sad

732
00:25:09.140 --> 00:25:12.380
but their affect is a bright smiling,

733
00:25:12.380 --> 00:25:15.900
laughing presentation while they discuss trauma.

734
00:25:15.900 --> 00:25:18.700
That mismatch is highly clinically significant.

735
00:25:18.720 --> 00:25:19.800
That's fascinating.

736
00:25:19.800 --> 00:25:21.440
The other big distinction in the MSE

737
00:25:21.440 --> 00:25:24.160
is thought process versus thought content.

738
00:25:24.160 --> 00:25:27.120
Process is the form or the shape of the thinking.

739
00:25:27.120 --> 00:25:28.040
Is it linear?

740
00:25:28.040 --> 00:25:30.140
Is it racing so fast they can't breathe?

741
00:25:30.140 --> 00:25:32.520
Or is it totally disorganized like a word salad?

742
00:25:32.520 --> 00:25:34.800
In content is the actual substance of the words.

743
00:25:34.800 --> 00:25:36.180
It's what they are thinking about.

744
00:25:36.180 --> 00:25:39.360
The delusions, the obsessions, the suicidal ideation.

745
00:25:39.360 --> 00:25:41.520
So you have to track both simultaneously.

746
00:25:41.520 --> 00:25:43.280
This raises an important question.

747
00:25:44.280 --> 00:25:46.560
The mental status exam requires the clinician

748
00:25:46.560 --> 00:25:48.700
to hold two realities at once.

749
00:25:48.700 --> 00:25:49.980
What the client is saying

750
00:25:49.980 --> 00:25:53.340
and how the client is fundamentally operating in space.

751
00:25:53.340 --> 00:25:55.100
It ensures they don't miss the truth

752
00:25:55.100 --> 00:25:56.540
hidden behind the symptoms.

753
00:25:56.540 --> 00:25:58.380
So what does this all mean?

754
00:25:58.380 --> 00:26:00.180
It's the ultimate test of paying attention

755
00:26:00.180 --> 00:26:01.780
and that really brings us full circle

756
00:26:01.780 --> 00:26:03.180
because everything we've talked about today

757
00:26:03.180 --> 00:26:05.720
proves that being well informed about mental health

758
00:26:05.720 --> 00:26:07.700
isn't just about memorizing symptom lists.

759
00:26:07.700 --> 00:26:08.540
Not at all.

760
00:26:08.540 --> 00:26:10.820
It's about paying incredibly close attention

761
00:26:10.820 --> 00:26:14.020
to context, to time, to the invisible targets of fear

762
00:26:14.020 --> 00:26:15.660
and to those hidden motives.

763
00:26:15.680 --> 00:26:18.120
But as we wrap up, I want to leave you listening at home

764
00:26:18.120 --> 00:26:20.640
with one final thought to mull over.

765
00:26:20.640 --> 00:26:23.240
We talked about how our current diagnostic system

766
00:26:23.240 --> 00:26:26.880
is heavily, almost rigidly reliant on timelines.

767
00:26:26.880 --> 00:26:29.240
Exactly one month to cross the boundary

768
00:26:29.240 --> 00:26:31.520
from acute stress to PTSD.

769
00:26:31.520 --> 00:26:33.080
Exactly six months to shift

770
00:26:33.080 --> 00:26:34.880
from schizophrenia to schizophrenia.

771
00:26:34.880 --> 00:26:36.080
But what happens to the person

772
00:26:36.080 --> 00:26:40.660
who is suffering profoundly on day 29 or on day 179?

773
00:26:40.660 --> 00:26:43.780
Does the human brain actually know what day it is?

774
00:26:43.780 --> 00:26:46.000
As we learn more about neurobiology,

775
00:26:46.000 --> 00:26:47.520
will the future of psychology

776
00:26:47.520 --> 00:26:50.520
eventually abandon these strict calendar-based labels

777
00:26:50.520 --> 00:26:52.360
entirely, moving toward a system

778
00:26:52.360 --> 00:26:54.400
that treats the individual engine of distress

779
00:26:54.400 --> 00:26:56.480
regardless of what the clock says?

780
00:26:56.480 --> 00:26:58.240
It's something to explore on your own.

781
00:26:58.240 --> 00:26:59.200
That's a profound question.

782
00:26:59.200 --> 00:27:02.160
We started out talking about the messy, murky reality

783
00:27:02.160 --> 00:27:03.720
of diagnosing the human mind

784
00:27:03.720 --> 00:27:05.140
where the X-ray machine is broken

785
00:27:05.140 --> 00:27:07.260
and all the boundaries are drawn in gray.

786
00:27:07.260 --> 00:27:09.460
But hopefully today's deep dive gave you a glimpse

787
00:27:09.460 --> 00:27:11.780
into exactly how professionals find clarity

788
00:27:11.780 --> 00:27:13.420
inside all that complexity.

789
00:27:13.420 --> 00:27:15.640
Thank you for joining us and stay curious.
